1. Assumptions & Daily Active Users (DAU)Registered Users: $10,000,000$ usersDaily Active Percentage: $10\%$ of registered users active dailyDaily Active Users (DAU):$$\text{DAU} = 10,000,000 \times 0.10 = 1,000,000\text{ users/day}$$User Behavior Assumptions:$1\text{ photo upload}$ per DAU per day$50\text{ feed page views}$ per DAU per day$1\text{ photo file size} = 2\text{ MB} = 2,000\text{ KB}$$1\text{ thumbnail size} = 50\text{ KB}$$1\text{ day} \approx 86,400\text{ seconds}$ (rounded to $100,000\text{ seconds}$ for back-of-the-envelope estimation, or $86,400$ for exact calculations; exact values used below).Peak Traffic Multiplier: $5\times$ average load.2. Capacity & Scale EstimatesA. Uploads Per SecondTotal Daily Uploads: $1,000,000\text{ uploads/day}$Average Uploads/sec:$$\text{Average Uploads/sec} = \frac{1,000,000}{86,400} \approx 11.57\text{ uploads/sec}$$Peak Uploads/sec ($5\times$):$$\text{Peak Uploads/sec} = 11.57 \times 5 \approx 57.87\text{ uploads/sec}$$B. Feed Views Per SecondTotal Daily Feed Views: $1,000,000 \text{ users} \times 50 \text{ views/user} = 50,000,000\text{ views/day}$Average Feed Views/sec:$$\text{Average Feed Views/sec} = \frac{50,000,000}{86,400} \approx 578.70\text{ views/sec}$$Peak Feed Views/sec ($5\times$):$$\text{Peak Feed Views/sec} = 578.70 \times 5 \approx 2,893.5\text{ views/sec}$$C. Photo Storage Per YearStorage per Photo (Original + Thumbnail):$$2.0\text{ MB} + 0.05\text{ MB} = 2.05\text{ MB/photo}$$Daily Storage Added:$$1,000,000 \times 2.05\text{ MB} = 2,050,000\text{ MB/day} = 2,050\text{ GB/day} = 2.05\text{ TB/day}$$Storage per Year ($365\text{ days}$):$$\text{Storage/Year} = 2.05\text{ TB/day} \times 365 \approx 748.25\text{ TB/year}$$3. Read-Heavy vs. Write-Heavy SystemSnapShare is a read-heavy system.Read to Write Ratio: $50,000,000\text{ feed views} : 1,000,000\text{ uploads} = 50:1$.Design Implications: Because read requests outweigh writes by 50 to 1, the architecture must prioritize fast read performance. This requires aggressive caching strategies (CDN for static images/thumbnails and Redis for user feed metadata), load balancing across stateless app servers, and database read replicas to offload read traffic from the primary database.4. Media Storage StrategyPhotos should not be stored as BLOBs inside a relational database because storing binary media bloats the database, degrades index performance, complicates database backups, and significantly increases storage costs.Instead, photo files should be stored in Cloud Object Storage (such as AWS S3, Google Cloud Storage, or MinIO), while the database stores only lightweight structured metadata (such as photo_id, user_id, s3_url, created_at, and caption).5. System Architecture Diagram                               +-------------------+
