6. Component ExplanationsCDN (Content Delivery Network): Caches static images and thumbnails on edge servers close to users to reduce latency and bandwidth usage on the origin server.Load Balancer: Distributes incoming application traffic evenly across stateless app servers to ensure high availability and eliminate single points of failure.App Servers: Executes stateless business logic, handles user authentication, and orchestrates database and storage operations.Cache (Redis / Memcached): Stores frequently read feed metadata and user sessions in memory to drastically lower response times for feed views.Primary Database (SQL/NoSQL): Handles write operations for metadata, user profiles, and social relationships to guarantee data integrity.Database Read Replica: Handles read queries for user profiles and feeds to offload traffic from the primary database.Object Storage (e.g., AWS S3): Provides scalable, highly durable blob storage for original high-resolution photo files and generated thumbnails.Message Queue (RabbitMQ / SQS): Decouples photo upload handling from background tasks by queuing thumbnail generation jobs asynchronously.Thumbnail Worker: Consumes tasks from the message queue to resize photos asynchronously without blocking the user's upload response.7. Step-by-Step Photo Upload FlowUpload Request: The client sends an HTTP POST request containing the image payload and metadata to the Load Balancer, which forwards it to an App Server.Direct Media Persist: The App Server writes the original full-size photo file directly to Object Storage and receives a unique file URL.Metadata Write: The App Server saves the photo metadata record (photo_id, user_id, object_storage_url, timestamp) into the Primary Database.Enqueue Asynchronous Job: The App Server pushes a create_thumbnail task containing the photo_id and original image URL into the Message Queue.Immediate Response: The App Server returns an HTTP 201 Success status code to the user without waiting for thumbnail generation to finish.Async Thumbnail Processing: A Thumbnail Worker consumes the task from the queue, downloads the photo from Object Storage, generates a 50 KB thumbnail, uploads the thumbnail back to Object Storage, and updates the database/cache with the thumbnail_url.8. Architectural Trade-offsEventual Consistency vs. Immediate Availability:Trade-off: Relying on asynchronous workers for thumbnail generation and database read replicas means a newly uploaded photo or feed item may take a brief moment (a few hundred milliseconds) to show up in a follower's feed.Justification: Prioritizes fast upload response times and system responsiveness over strict immediate consistency.Push vs. Pull Feed Architecture (Fan-out on Write vs. Read):Trade-off: Pre-computing and caching feeds when a user posts (push/fan-out on write) consumes high storage and queue resources for users with thousands of followers. Conversely, building feeds on demand (pull/fan-out on read) simplifies writes but increases database read latency.Justification: Using a hybrid approach (push for regular users, pull for high-follower celebrity accounts) optimizes overall system resource usage and cache efficiency.
